# DAS Tool 1.1.7, verified 2026-09-11, digest
# sha256:8a523dd78e9b2de9a0e9548cda91e066dee6c6cb5297e5a288150f19315839c8.
# `DAS_Tool --version` answers `DAS Tool 1.1.7`, and diamond, pullseq and ruby
# are all on PATH beside it -- the R driver shells out to them itself, so this
# one image covers the whole transform. search_engine defaults to diamond, not
# the USEARCH the paper's own results were generated with (Methods, Sieber
# et al. 2018): USEARCH is not on PATH here, so diamond is not a choice made
# by this file, it is the only search engine the image can run.
#
# It also carries the SCG reference: /usr/local/share/das_tool-1.1.7-1/db/
# holds {bac,arc}.{all,scg}.faa and {bac,arc}.scg.lookup, ~83 MB total, which
# is what --dbDirectory defaults to and what the paper's "51 bacterial or 38
# archaeal reference single-copy genes" (Methods) are drawn from. No separate
# reference input or download step is needed. Checked by running `DAS_Tool -h`
# and inspecting that directory inside the image as a non-root user, then a
# real 3-binner consolidation with `-p` externally-predicted proteins against
# a 541k-contig/830k-ORF CAMI assembly (real MetaBAT2/SemiBin2/COMEBin output
# from fir run VTuXlulT) -- 92s wall on 16 threads, non-empty bins written.
container: docker://quay.io/biocontainers/das_tool:1.1.7--r44hdfd78af_1
conda: das_tool
